Decentralized MARL for Networked Agents under Non-Stationarity

Problem Statement: In decentralized MARL, each agent observes only local information while
all other agents are simultaneously updating their policies (πt
j → πt+1
j ), making the effective en-
vironment non-stationary from any single agent’s perspective. In networked/physical settings this
compounds with limited neighbors and limited communication.

Research Direction: How can decentralized agents learn stable cooperative policies when
they only observe local neighbors and the policies of neighboring agents continuously change?

• Bayesian Ego-Graph Inference for Networked Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning — NeurIPS
2025 (introduces BayesG; scales to 167 agents on traffic-control tasks; official code available)
• COGNAC: Cooperative Graph-Based Networked Agent Challenges for MARL — NeurIPS 2025,
Datasets & Benchmarks Track (graph-structured benchmark; official code, pip-installable)
• Distributed Entropy-Regularized Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning with Policy Consensus —
Automatica, 2024 (theoretical convergence analysis; MPE experiments)
• Decentralized Multi-Agent Cooperation via Adaptive Partner Modeling — Complex & Intelligent
Systems, 2024
• Open Ad Hoc Teamwork with Cooperative Game Theory — ICML 2024 (introduces CIAO; official
code available; useful for an unseen-teammate extension)

Suggested Project: Vary agent count N ∈ {5, 10, 25, 50, 100} and network topology (ring →
grid → random graph → scale-free graph), and communication radius k = 1, 2, 3, . . . neighbor
hops. Introduce non-stationarity by switching a subset of agents’ policies mid-training (t < tc : πA,
t ≥ tc : πB ). Measure convergence speed, reward, instability, adaptation speed, communication
cost, and scalability. Possible Extensions / Improvements: Prefer this framing over simply
“implement BayesG” (pure reproduction); instead use COGNAC’s configurable graph structure
and BayesG’s released code together so students can vary topology themselves and report how
stability degrades as a function of topology and neighborhood size
